<Base title="About" description="Brandon Fox: Lead Software Engineer at Capital One by day, backend and platform engineering, Axiom-Driven Engineering on my own time.">
  <article class="article">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      I am a backend and platform engineer on Long Island, New York. By day I am a Lead Software Engineer at Capital One, on the individual-contributor track, working on backend and infrastructure problems at the architecture level. The projects and writing on this site are what I do on my own time, with my own equipment, on my own account.
    </p>
    <p>
      The thread through all of it is correctness you can check. I would rather write the rule down and let a machine enforce it than remember to be careful. That is where Axiom-Driven Engineering comes from: a small set of falsifiable rules, each with a check, run on every commit. The <a href="/#method">method page</a> lists the current nine; <a href="https://github.com/First-ADE">First-ADE</a> holds the code.
    </p>
    <p>
      I work spec-first. The specification is the source of truth; the code is what the specification reproduces. When the two disagree, the spec is what I fix first, and the traceability table in <a href="https://github.com/brandon-fox/alos">alos</a> is where I keep myself honest about how much of the spec the code has actually caught up to.
    </p>
    <p>
      I am drawn to staff-level platform work: the shape of a system over years, the invariants that make it safe to change, and the tooling that lets many people change it without breaking each other. If you are working on that kind of problem, I would like to hear about it.
    </p>

